          "text": "## Verdict first\n\n**Split it.** Ship reasoning effort alone. Defer `temperature`/`topP`/`topK` — they're a different product question and llame has no generation-parameter seam at all today.\n**Add the cache flag.** The evidence is strong and it genuinely can't be hardcoded.\n\n---\n\n## Three corrections to your premise\n\n**1. The pinned SDK has no top-level `reasoning` param.** I checked the installed types, not the docs:\n\n```\nai@6.0.217 → CallSettings = { maxOutputTokens, temperature, topP, topK,\n                              presencePenalty, frequencyPenalty,\n                              stopSequences, seed, maxRetries,\n                              abortSignal, timeout, headers }\n                              ^ no `reasoning`\n@ai-sdk/openai@3.0.79 → reasoningEffort on BOTH option types:\n   openaiLanguageModelChatOptions:      \"none\"|\"minimal\"|\"low\"|\"medium\"|\"high\"|\"xhigh\"\n   openaiLanguageModelResponsesOptions: string\n```\n\nThe `reasoning: 'high'` portable parameter in the docs you linked is newer than the catalog pin. So \"don't use the AI SDK standardized approach\" is currently *forced*, not a choice — you'd wire `providerOptions.openai.reasoningEffort` inside `openai-model-client.ts`. Good news: it's on the chat-completions options too, so both llame paths (`openai.chat()` for compatible endpoints, `openai()` Responses for native) take it.\n\nDesign implication: keep llame's internal effort value as a **provider-neutral enum**, mapped to `providerOptions` in each client. That's the same shape the SDK will eventually give you for free, so bumping `ai` later becomes a one-line swap in the client, not a config migration.\n\n**2. `'max'` isn't real for you.** `'max'` exists only in Anthropic's native `providerOptions.anthropic.effort`. llame's `ProviderType` enum is strict-closed on `'openai'` — there is no Anthropic adapter. The portable set is `none|minimal|low|medium|high|xhigh`. You dropped `minimal` and added `max`; both wrong for today.\n\n**3. `models[].reasoning: boolean` already exists** — in `llame.config.schema.json`, `RawModelEntry`, `PublicModelCatalogEntry`, and `AvailableModelResponse`. Your object replaces it. Under your no-backcompat rule that's correct, but it's a coordinated delta across `available-models` + `reasoning-output` specs, and boot must fail loudly on the old boolean shape rather than coerce. Also: drop `available` from the object — presence *is* the flag.\n\n```jsonc\n\"reasoning\": {\n  \"effortLevels\": [\"low\", \"medium\", \"high\", \"xhigh\"],  // ordered, non-empty\n  \"defaultEffort\": \"high\",                              // must ∈ effortLevels\n  \"effortInvalidatesCache\": true                        // see below\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Where it lands\n\n```\nPOST /chats/:id/messages           CreateMessageDto\n  { modelId, effort?, message }         + effort?: enum          ← 1 field\n        │\n        ▼\n  ChatLoopService.send                                  ← resolve defaultEffort HERE\n        │  validate: model has reasoning{}? effort ∈ effortLevels? → 422\n        │\n        ├─ runs.insert({ modelId, effort, ... })        ← NEW COLUMN, mirrors modelId\n        │\n        ├─ model_context_snapshots  ✗ effort does NOT go here\n        └─ context items (model-change notice)  ✗ no effort notice\n        │\n        ▼\n  RunExecutionService.execute\n        │  client.streamText({ ..., effort })           ← ModelStreamInput += effort\n        ▼\n  openai-model-client.streamText\n        streamOptions.providerOptions = {\n          openai: { ...(nativeOpenAI && {reasoningSummary:'auto'}),\n                    ...(effort && {reasoningEffort: effort}) } }\n```\n\n**Resolve the default at accept time and store the effective value.** The `runs.model_id` comment already states the principle verbatim: *\"changing the system default later must not silently alter an already queued run.\"* Same argument, same column style. Don't store null-meaning-default.\n\n**Effort must NOT enter `model_context_snapshots`.** That table is content-addressed and deliberately reused across runs with identical prompt/tools; effort varies per turn under exactly those conditions. This is the same reasoning the schema already spells out for why `runs.context_items` isn't in there. Putting effort in the receipt would shatter snapshot reuse for zero benefit.\n\n**No context-rail notice for effort changes.** `EFFECTIVE_CONTEXT_CHANGE_CAUSES` is a closed union currently `['model']`, and someone will be tempted to add `'effort'`. Resist: a model switch changes *who is reading the history* and needs telling; an effort change doesn't alter what the model knows. It's a cost/UX concern, not a context event.\n\n**Expose it on `RunResponse`** (which already carries `modelId`) and in assistant usage telemetry. \"UI is separate\" is fine, but `lastRun.effort != newEffort` is exactly the comparison your warning needs, and the API is the only thing that can supply it.\n\n**Post-turn calls need an explicit answer.** Compaction currently uses the triggering run's model id (per `available-models` spec). Does it inherit effort? Recommend **no** — effort is a per-turn user intent about the answer, and summarization on `xhigh` is pure waste. Title generation likewise. Name this in the proposal; leaving it implicit means someone threads it through by accident.\n\n---\n\n## Why sampling params are a separate (and later) proposal\n\n```\n                    effort                   temperature/topP/topK\nlifecycle      per-request, user-chosen  →   per-model or per-instance, operator\nUI surface     composer control          →   nothing user-facing planned\nSOTA models    the primary control       →   rejected outright (gpt-5.x: 400)\nseam today     none                      →   none\ncache impact   documented, real          →   none\npersistence    needs a run column        →   arguably doesn't\n```\n\nTwo things worth knowing:\n\n- **`runs.maxOutputTokens` is never sent to the provider.** It's read once in `run-execution.service.ts:638` purely as a context-window-fit reservation. So llame has *zero* generation parameters reaching the model. Whichever proposal lands first pays the seam cost of adding them to `ModelStreamInput`.\n- **The AI SDK already warns, doesn't throw**, on unsupported settings — `result.warnings`. But OpenAI's API returns a hard 400 for `temperature` on gpt-5 reasoning models, so the warning won't save you; you'd need per-model declaration of which sampling params are legal. That's a bigger config surface than effort for less value.\n\nSampling params are YAGNI until someone actually asks. If they land, they're operator-static per-model config (`models[].sampling: { temperature: 0.7 }`), not a per-request field.\n\n---\n\n## The cache flag: research says yes\n\nThis is the strongest part of your instinct, and better-supported than you probably expected.\n\n**Anthropic documents it explicitly**, in the prompt-caching invalidation table:\n\n> \"Changing the `output_config.effort` value **always invalidates message blocks**, with the same model-specific effect on tool and system caches as thinking parameters. **Setting effort explicitly to the model's default is equivalent to omitting it and does not invalidate.**\"\n\nAnd for thinking params: *\"tool and system caches are also invalidated **on models that render the configuration ahead of them**\"* — i.e. the blast radius is **per-model**, which is precisely why this can't be a hardcoded constant.\n\n**OpenAI does it too, undocumented.** [openai/codex#35416](https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/35416) reports a full-prefix miss on every effort change inside a session, order-independent — *except* that **returning to a previously-used level hits the old cache**. The reporter also notes Claude Code shows exactly the warning you're describing on some models, and that **Haiku 4.5 showed no miss at all** — model-specific, again.\n\nSo: operator-declared per-model flag, correct. But the warning condition is subtler than `last != new`:\n\n```\nwarn IF  model.reasoning.effortInvalidatesCache\n    AND  newEffort != lastRunEffort\n    AND  newEffort ∉ effortLevelsAlreadyUsedInThisChat   ← OpenAI: revisit = hit\n```\n\nThe third clause needs the per-run effort history, which you get free from the `runs` column. Whether the API computes it or ships the raw history to the client is a UI-proposal call — but the column has to exist either way, which is another argument for landing effort persistence in *this* proposal.\n\nOne caveat on scope: llame doesn't currently set `cacheControl` or `promptCacheKey` anywhere; you're riding OpenAI's *implicit* caching. So the flag buys you a cost warning today, not a correctness behavior. That's still worth it — it's the difference between a user idly toggling effort and understanding they just paid for a full re-read.\n\n---\n\n## Loose ends worth a decision\n\n- **Misdeclared levels on compatible endpoints.** An operator lists `xhigh` for a model that doesn't support it → provider 400 → run failure. Consistent with the existing \"provider credential validity is not prevalidated\" posture, but say so out loud in the spec rather than letting it be discovered.\n- **`'none'` is a level, not an absence.** `reasoningEffort: 'none'` on GPT-5.1+ means *disable reasoning*, which is different from omitting the parameter. If `'none'` is in `effortLevels`, it must be sent, not dropped by a truthiness check. Easy bug.\n- **Ordering.** `effortLevels` should be an ordered array (config order = UI order), not a set. Free, and it saves the UI proposal from inventing a sort.\n\n---\n\nWant me to turn this into an OpenSpec change (`add-reasoning-effort`, touching `instance-config` + `available-models` + the `reasoning-output` boolean removal), or keep pulling on any of these threads?",
