            "content": "Layer 4 of the reasoning-effort stack. Accept, validate, persist, and send a\nper-request effort — the first generation parameter llame has ever passed to a\nprovider.\n\n```\nPOST /chats/:id/messages  { modelId, effort?, message }\n      │  model resolves first; effort is never evaluated without one\n      ▼\n  runs.effort  (nullable text, resolved at accept time, stored concretely)\n      │  worker reads it inside the claim; never re-resolves\n      ▼\n  providerOptions.openai.reasoningEffort   (Responses AND Chat Completions)\n```\n\n## Validation\n\n`effort` is matched **byte-exactly** against the selected model's declared\n`effortLevels`. Nothing is trimmed, case-folded, or normalized, so `High`\nagainst a declared `high` is simply not a match — casing falls out of \"no\nnormalization\" rather than being a rule of its own. Omitting it resolves the\nmodel's `defaultEffort`; a model that declares no vocabulary accepts no effort\nat all.\n\nFailures are 422 `effort_not_available`, alongside the existing\n`model_not_available` in the same envelope, discriminated by `code`.\n\n**Model resolution runs first, structurally.** `resolveEffortSelection` takes\nthe already-validated catalog entry rather than a model id, so an unavailable\nmodel cannot reach effort validation at all — a level's legality is meaningless\nwithout a resolved model. A test asserts the resolver is never called when the\nmodel lookup throws.\n\n## Persistence\n\n`runs.effort` stores the **concrete resolved value**, not a marker meaning \"use\nthe default\" — the rule `runs.model_id` already states: a later configuration\nedit must not alter an already queued run. Nullable, with no literal backfill:\na run predating the feature genuinely had no effort, unlike `model_id`, which\nexecution cannot proceed without.\n\nThe worker reads it inside the claim transaction rather than from the queue\npayload (one source of truth) and **never re-resolves or re-validates it**. A\nlevel the operator has since withdrawn from `effortLevels` is still sent\nverbatim. The integration suite pins exactly that: a run stored at a level no\nmodel declares still executes at it.\n\n## Wire mapping\n\nLives in `openai-model-client.ts`, not the executor, so a future Anthropic\nadapter maps the same neutral string onto `providerOptions.anthropic.effort`\nwith no executor change. Merged with the existing native `reasoningSummary`\nrather than replacing it, and gated on `effort !== undefined` — never\ntruthiness, because a level denoting disabled reasoning is a real instruction\nand differs from omitting the parameter.\n\n## One thing worth reviewing\n\n`FakeModelsService` and `ScriptedModelsService` are injected via Nest overrides,\nwhich are **not** structurally typechecked — so adding a method to\n`ModelSelectionValidator` compiled clean and surfaced as 22 integration failures\nwith `resolveEffortSelection is not a function`. Both now declare\n`implements ModelSelectionValidator`, which turns that class of mistake into a\ncompile error. Both also delegate to the production resolver rather than\nrestating its rules, so a suite cannot pass while the real API accepts a\ndifferent set of levels.\n\n## Not in this layer\n\nCompaction inheritance, telemetry, the context receipt, and the\n`model.requested` event payload — all of `effort/disclosure`, the layer above.\n\n## Verification\n\n`turbo run build --filter=api`, `api lint`, `api typecheck`, `api test`\n(1522 passed), `api test:integration`, `web build`. `openapi.json` and the Orval\nbindings are regenerated and committed. The migration is purely\ndrizzle-generated (nullable additive column; `runs` is already FORCE RLS) and\n`drizzle-kit check` passes.\n",
